# Documentation Authoring

- Write hand-authored framework documentation under `docs/sphinx/source/` using the repository's MyST Markdown conventions.
- Do not manually edit `docs/sphinx/source/code/**`; those API reference inventory pages are generated from source.
- Put authoritative API details in Python docstrings and JavaScript JSDoc so generated reference pages remain synchronized with implementation.
- Every hand-authored `addon-*.md` page for an addon that registers blueprints or generated CRUD must include an `## HTTP API` section. Document method, canonical `/api/...` path, auth, request params/body, and response shape; verify each row against current blueprint and model source. Point integrators at `api-integration.md` for shared session, CSRF, error envelope, and generated CRUD verbs — do not duplicate the full CRUD verb matrix on every addon page.
- Add new hand-authored pages to the appropriate `toctree` in `docs/sphinx/source/index.md` or a relevant section index.
- Separate normative contracts from orientation, examples, compatibility notes, and future operational proposals. Use requirement language only for behavior enforced by current code, tests, or policy.
- Verify framework claims against current runtime wiring and representative tests. Do not copy historical examples into guidance without checking that they remain supported.
- Describe cross-runtime payloads, configuration precedence, restart requirements, security implications, and failure behavior explicitly when relevant.
- Use repository-relative links and existing anchors. Keep terminology consistent with the framework: runtime, addon, component identity, payload field, renderer, pack, worker, and beat.
- Never include real secrets, private endpoints, tokens, or production credentials in examples.
- Regenerate reference pages through the documentation build workflow instead of copying or hand-maintaining generated output. Do not invent or hand-edit generated pages under `docs/sphinx/source/code/**`.
- Edit hand-authored sources under `docs/sphinx/source/` as needed. Do not run `scripts/docs/build_docs.py`, `docs/sphinx/build-docs.sh`, render HTML, regenerate API reference inventories, or start/recreate the `docs` service in `docker/application/docs.yaml` unless the user explicitly asks. Leave Sphinx build, docs site refresh, and link validation to humans.
